---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { brainBudgetRows, brainCommandRows, memoryRoleRows } from "../../data/docs-content"
import { release111 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="brain">

          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.110, Vector keeps a brain for each repository and one for you: the rules you write, what
            you tell it about yourself, what it learns while it works, and the documents you give it. All of it is
            stored and searched on this computer. Beyond your rules, the start of <code>MEMORY.md</code> and the entries
            you pinned, which go with every request but a greeting, Vector sends your model only the few lines that bear
            on each request. Private documents never go to a free model labelled <strong>May train on your
            prompts</strong>, or to a session a guest is in.
          </p>
          <p>
            Desktop 1.99.109 and earlier keep one <code>MEMORY.md</code> file, shown in Settings → General, and send it
            with every request.
          </p>

          <h2 id="what-it-holds">What the brain holds</h2>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>Rules</dt><dd>The repository's <code>.vector/RULES.md</code> and your own <code>RULES.md</code> in Vector's config folder, beside the <code>AGENTS.md</code> that <a href="/docs/vector-agent#repository-rules">Repository rules</a> describes. Only you write rules; Vector never adds one on its own.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Your memory</dt><dd><code>MEMORY.md</code> in the same folder, and what you ask Vector to remember about you. It applies in every repository.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>What Vector learned</dt><dd>Short lessons, decisions and preferences the agent saved while it worked, each with the evidence it was saved from.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Documents</dt><dd>Specs, PDFs, runbooks and API descriptions you add, and the repository's own docs. See <a href="/docs/documents">Documents and code links</a>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>A repository's <code>BRAIN.md</code></dt><dd>Memory a repository ships with it. Vector uses it once you trust the repository; until then it waits for your review.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Files you write stay yours: Vector only reads them. What it learns and what it reads from documents live in
            <code>brain.db</code> in Vector's data folder, beside its sessions. A key pasted into a memory or rules file
            is not sent to a provider, and Vector removes what looks like a secret from what it reads of a document
            before saving it. A copy kept with <a href="/docs/documents#adding">Keep copies of added files</a> is the
            file as you added it, secrets included.
          </p>

          <h2 id="requests">What a request carries</h2>
          <p>
            Every request but a greeting carries your rules, the start of <code>MEMORY.md</code> (about 800 tokens of
            it), the entries you pinned (about 300 tokens) and one line saying what else the brain holds. That part stays the same through a
            session, so the provider's prompt cache can reuse it. Then each request message recalls only what bears on
            it, within a budget set by the request's size, and recalls nothing when nothing does:
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {brainBudgetRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
          <p>
            A greeting or a bare acknowledgement, such as "hi" or "ok", is sized Chat unless work is waiting on you: one
            that answers a question the agent asked, follows a run that did not finish, or approves a plan from Plan
            mode is sized as the work it carries on. A Chat request carries no memory, no memory instructions and no
            memory tool, so it costs no extra tokens. Reading a file brings in up to about 200
            tokens of memory about that file, and the agent can search memory and open an entry with its memory tool
            when it decides to look. When a run nears its spend limit, a repository with documents recalls half as much.
          </p>

          <h2 id="learning">Learning as it works</h2>
          <p>
            Vector saves a short lesson, decision or preference as it works: when you state one, when a fix makes a
            failing check pass, or when a lasting decision is made. It is in use from the next request. Each one shows
            as a Learned line in the session and under <strong>Recently learned</strong> in Settings → Memory, where one
            click undoes it; after Undo, Vector does not save the same words, or a close rewording, for 180 days. It
            saves at most three a request message.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it learns in a session that read web pages or other outside content, ran MCP tools, or had a guest or
            a subagent in it waits for your review instead. Turn learning off with <strong>Let Vector learn as it
            works</strong> in Settings → Memory, or <code>vector memory learning off</code>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="about-you">Learning about you</h2>
          <p>
            {release111.since} Vector learns from what you type: the formats and kinds of data you like, what you mean by
            words you use, and how you like things done. "When I say ship it, I mean merge and deploy" teaches a term,
            which comes into a later request only when that request uses the words. "I prefer tables", "always run the
            tests first" or "never force-push to main" teaches a preference. It reads only your own typed words, never
            text you paste, quote or put in code, and costs no model call. A short statement in a short message is in use
            at once; anything longer waits for a second sighting or one click. <em>Vector tries not to keep sensitive
            personal details:</em> a sentence that tells it about your health, money, religion, politics, relationships,
            sexuality or gender is dropped.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each item shows under the reply as <em>Learned from what you typed: "…"</em>, with Undo, and under
            <strong>Settings → Memory → About you</strong>, grouped by kind: Formats, Workflow, What you mean, Tools,
            Sources and Style. Each item shows the words it came from; edit, undo or forget it, or turn a kind off.
            <strong>Erase what Vector learned in this session</strong> forgets everything whose only evidence is that
            session, and the Deep research runs it started. When your words teach nothing the rules can keep, such as a correction ("no, I meant…"), a small
            model can suggest what to learn: <strong>Let a model help Vector learn</strong> runs it with Vector Local
            only (the default), with any model, or never. Its tokens count in that session. Up to 300 entries about you
            stay in use; past that, tidying archives the ones used least.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Erase removes it from the brain and stops Vector using it. The sessions it came from keep their messages
            until you delete them. A model server you host may keep its own logs.</em>
          </p>

          <h2 id="proven">What proved useful</h2>
          <p>
            A card or memory entry that an answer cited, or that the agent opened, in a run whose checks went from
            failing to passing (or that the judge passed) gains weight. A record cited in a run you reverted loses it,
            and one given to the agent again and again without being used fades. The weight moves a candidate's value
            by at most 30% either way, and the counts behind it halve every 90 days, so it reorders what recall picks
            but never decides it alone. A session a guest is in, or that took in outside content, credits nothing.
          </p>

          <h2 id="subagents-and-review">Subagents and Vectorscope</h2>
          <p>
            In desktop 1.99.110, a subagent started by a Thorough request begins with up to 200 tokens of the cards and
            memory that bear on its task, for up to four subagents a request, and none for smaller requests.
            {release111.since} every subagent also gets how you work, as <a href="#every-agent">Memory of you in every
            agent</a> describes. With the next CLI release, a
            <a href="/docs/vectorscope">Vectorscope</a> review you run in the terminal with <code>vector review</code>
            gets the requirements, constraints, contracts and decisions linked to the changed files, with any drift, in
            at most 300 tokens: private documents only when the review model is not a free model labelled <strong>May
            train on your prompts</strong>, never in a
            review posted to a pull request, and never content that waits for your review. Vectorscope reviews in the
            desktop app do not get them yet.
          </p>

          <h2 id="every-agent">Memory of you in every agent</h2>
          <p>
            {release111.since} every agent uses what Vector has learned about you: coding, subagents, the Vectorscope
            reviews you run in Vector and Deep research. Sessions with invited guests, and free models that may train on
            your prompts, get none of it. Reviews and research see how you work, not facts about you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How you work</strong> is your preferences, decisions and lessons, the terms Vector learned and your
            own <code>RULES.md</code>. <strong>Facts about you</strong> are the facts you or Vector saved about you,
            <code>MEMORY.md</code>, private documents, and anything learned in a session that read a private document.
            For a role whose output leaves this computer, a decision or a lesson goes only when it names nothing in
            particular: no name, address, host, amount, date or long number.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {memoryRoleRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt>{term}</dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
          <p>
            The lines each role gets come out of its own small budget, and a greeting still carries none. Reviews that run
            in GitHub Actions have no brain, because <code>brain.db</code> is on your computer.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before Vector posts a pull request or a review, or makes a commit, it checks the text against what it knows
            about you. Text Vector or an agent wrote loses a sentence that repeats it, after you see what goes: post
            without it, post as is, or cancel. Text you typed or edited is never changed; Vector shows the overlap and
            asks. When the check cannot run, Vector asks whether to post anyway. Commands the agent runs itself, such as
            <code>git commit -m</code> or <code>gh pr create</code>, are not checked.
          </p>

          <h2 id="settings">Settings → Memory</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Memory</strong> lists this repository's entries or your own: search them, add one, edit, pin or
            forget it, and see what waits for your review. <strong>Recently learned</strong> lists what Vector saved on
            its own, newest first, with Undo. <strong>Tidy and erase</strong> merges duplicates, archives what goes
            unused and erases what Vector learned, leaving the files you wrote alone. <strong>Documents</strong> is
            described in <a href="/docs/documents">Documents and code links</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release111.since} <strong>About you</strong> lists what Vector learned about you, as described above, and
            <strong>Research</strong> lists this repository's <a href="/docs/research">Deep research</a> runs.
            <strong>Export and import</strong> saves what Vector keeps as Markdown to read or JSON to bring to another
            computer, for you, this repository or both. <em>Imported items wait for your approval</em>, and
            <strong>Approve all</strong> puts them in use in one click.
          </p>

          <h2 id="terminal">In the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector memory</code> manages the same brain. The npm CLI 1.99.99 does not have it; it comes with the
            next CLI release, and Windows WSL servers get it once a later desktop release requires that CLI. Add
            <code>--personal</code> for your own memory.
          </p>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            {brainCommandRows.map(([term, detail]) => (<div><dt><code>{term}</code></dt><dd>{detail}</dd></div>))}
          </dl>
          <p class="note">
            Deleting your Vector account does not reach the brain, because it is on your computer. Erase what Vector
            learned and the documents it keeps with <code>vector memory erase --everywhere --yes</code>; the files you
            wrote stay until you delete them.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
